  "market_context": "Downtrend in GER40 where price briefly spiked above a prior rejection wick (liquidity grab), then resumed downward. The analyst emphasized this occurred during a strong bearish market structure with consecutive lower lows.",
  "key_observation": "Price grabbed liquidity above a previous wick and continued down; the bottom of the downtrend showed no regular bearish divergence—instead, RSI made lower lows matching price, confirming convergence and trend continuation.",
  "analysis_steps": [
    "Step 1: Identified a liquidity grab above a prior rejection wick during a downtrend.",
    "Step 2: Observed that price made consecutive lower lows while RSI also made lower lows—no regular bearish divergence formed.",
    "Step 3: Concluded this was convergence (not divergence), validating the ongoing downtrend and warning against counter-trend regular divergence trades.",
    "Step 4: Contrasted this with proper hidden divergences that align with trend and PD array zones for high-probability entries."
  ],
  "outcome": null,
  "lessons": "Counter-trend regular divergences are unreliable when price shows convergence (RSI matching price direction); always confirm with market structure—liquidity grabs can trap traders expecting reversals.",
